  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nWe focus on two main algorithmic improvements: stochastic gradient-based optimization to escape local minima and dimensionality reduction via piecewise constant function approximation. These changes aim to lower the current upper bound by systematically exploring more complex function shapes and reducing computational bottlenecks. Unlike the previous local-first approach, we prioritize stochastic exploration to maintain global optimality while falling back to gradient-based methods.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - The objective is to minimize:\n        C = (1/\u0394x) * max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n     where \u0394x=2/n_points is the bin width, and the convolution is computed via cross-correlation.\n\n   - Representations: \n        Base representation: n=336 (interpolate from parent)\n        Higher resolution: n=672\n        Intermediate resolution: n=168\n\n   - Constraints: sum(h) = n/2, 0\u2264h\u22641.\n\n   - Stochastic Optimization:\n        Objective surrogate: To stabilize large correlations, use a smoothed objective function:\n           f(h) = max_k [E_\u03c4 [ sum_i h[i] (1 - h[i+k + \u03c4]) ]]\n        where \u03c4 is a uniform perturbation in [0,10] dx.\n\n        For large-scale computation, approximate by sampling 10\u03c4 independently.\n\n   - Dimensionality Reduction:\n        For n=672, project the function into a basis of piecewise constant functions on reduced intervals (say, 112 segments) to lower dimension while maintaining key harmonic structure.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n\n   a. Dependencies:\n        Use scipy, numpy, and heapq for priority queue.\n\n   b. Main phases:\n\n        i. Low-resolution initialization:\n            Start with interpolated parent (n=336) projected to n=336 (clip and scale).\n\n        ii. Stochastic gradient phase:\n            For each resolution (336, 168, 672):\n\n                A. Forward pass on base grid:\n                    Compute candidate function, its neighborhood offsets, and apply the smoothing trick.\n\n                B. Stochastic gradients:\n                    Perturb the domain randomly and compute gradient steps using sampled neighbors.\n\n                C. Adaptive learning rate:\n                    Use RMSProp with decay by sqrt(time) for long-term stability.\n\n            Implement within `run`:\n\n                deadlines = [t + 300, t + 800, t + 1200]  # three phases\n\n                Schedule across resolutions: {336: early, 672: middle}\n\n            Include fallback to SLSQP if gradient-free methods are needed.\n\n        iii. Dimensionality reduction for n=672:\n            Divide the 672 points into m=112 segments (each of length 672/m), maintain one parameter per segment.\n            Compute average value, and project the optimization accordingly.\n\n            Formally, define h_seg = average in segment, then the quart integral constraint becomes sum(h_seg * segment_length / delta) = n/2.\n\n        iv. Candidate selection:\n            After each iteration, select best candidate by:\n                exact_c5 = evaluate_c5(...)\n                if exact_c5 < current_best: record\n\n            Return: h_final = projected version for 336 points.\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n\n        i. Base resolution (n=336):\n            Num_steps = min(int((deadline-first_phase)/0.1), 500)\n            SGD parameters: lr_initial=0.02, decay=0.995, batch_size=64\n\n        ii. Higher resolution (n=672):\n            Effective dimensions = ceil(sqrt(n)) = 26\n            Sample directions from sphere method to focus exploration in high-dimensional space.\n\n        iii. Early stopping criteria:\n            If no improvement in last 10 steps and entire dataset is stuck, switch to interpolation-based restart.\n\n        iv. Other variations:\n            - Temperature schedule for gradient steps (to allow occasional upward moves)\n            - Maintain a population of candidates and k-armed bandits to choose direction\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n\n        Estimate time per resolution:\n\n            n=336: O(10^6) operations if done naively, SGD should be cheaper.\n            n=672: 4x more operations, but dimensionality reduction cuts this by 4x.\n\n        Use exponential backoff in SGD hyperparameters if iteration count shows failure.\n\n        For initial implementation:\n            resolution_backoff = {336: [336, 672, 168], 672: [672, 336, 168], ...} \n            Diversify resolutions unless deadline hazard.\n\n        Fallback procedure for invalid candidates:\n            Use random feasible function at local resolution (n//4 or something)\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n\n        For every candidate (even after projection):\n            Check: y = -evaluate_c5(x) (minimization of C)\n            If y < best and candidate satisfies constraints (project if needed)\n\n        Final candidate must be represented as:\n            x_final = best_h_selens (projected to n=336)\n            evaluate_c5(x_final) < current_best\n\n        Errors: \n          - if deadline exceeds - return best_valid\n          - if dimensione discrepancies use interator interpolation.",
